{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrkQG93BASY2XkDRMHj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X6MbmA8BHAcxhDsf8roob8","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050554__x4cdb93/work/PLAN.md","offset":380,"limit":420},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13269,"cache_read_input_tokens":24346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13269},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3fa61e8-1431-4900-ae89-edc59162574b","uuid":"0181a30c-fa86-4c5a-972a-501b5d93e831","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:06:04.761Z","request_id":"req_011CghrkPzWtJWhcFynjNXL2","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01X6MbmA8BHAcxhDsf8roob8":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050554__x4cdb93/work/PLAN.md","offset":380,"limit":420}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X6MbmA8BHAcxhDsf8roob8","type":"tool_result","content":"380\t**Projection** (a sheared perspective: verticals stay vertical, which suits billboards and the column renderer):\n381\t- Camera `{ x, y, z, yaw, pitch, fov }` (fov = degrees across the screen's height; pitch + = looking down).\n382\t- Screen `W x H`, centre `cx = W/2`, `cy = H/2`; focal `f = (H/2) / tan(fov/2)`; `horizonY = cy - f * tan(pitch)`.\n383\t- Forward `F = (cos yaw, sin yaw)`, right `R = (-sin yaw, cos yaw)`.\n384\t- `project(P)`: `d = P - C`; `D = d.F` (forward distance); `L = d.R`; null if `D < 1 m`; `k = f / D` (pixels per unit);\n385\t  `sx = cx + L*k`; `sy = horizonY - (P.z - C.z)*k`. Returns `[sx, sy, k, D]`.\n386\t- `ray(sx, sy)`: direction `dir = F + R*((sx - cx)/f) + up*((horizonY - sy)/f)`; the point at forward distance D is\n387\t  `C + D*dir`. Shots, picking and the bot all use this.\n388\t- **Magnifying about a point** (the scope lens): the same camera with focal `f*mag`, centred so screen point `s0` lands\n389\t  on the lens centre: `s' = c_lens + (s - s0)*mag`. The lens is an exact zoom of the main view, so the shot through its\n390\t  centre is the main view's ray through `s0`.\n391\t- **Rigs and props** are drawn as orthographic billboards: an `E.View('vista', 'Vista', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)` with\n392\t  `yawDeg = -yaw*180/pi - 90` (Mode 7's formula) and `pitchDeg` = the angle from the camera down to the object,\n393\t  clamped to 4..30 degrees, quantized to 2 degrees; views cached by (k in 1/32 steps below 1, 1/16 above; yaw in 2\n394\t  degree steps; pitch).\n395\t\n396\t**Director** (per site, deterministic; keyed to site time, never to player input, so all players and all AI Hub copies\n397\tsee the same frame): keyframes `[t, { bearing, pitch, fov, ease, dur }]`; optional `follow` mode in single player\n398\t(yaw eases toward the centroid of live bucks, clamped to the site's bearing range, smoothed over 0.8 s). Single player\n399\talso gets a 0.4 degree recoil kick per shot (recovers in 0.25 s) and a slow breathing drift (0.15 degrees); local\n400\tmultiplayer and hub rounds use keyframes only and no kick. Canoe vantage: a gentle bob (z +-4 cm, roll +-0.6 degrees,\n401\tperiod 3.1 s). Walk-ins: the camera follows an `E.Path` along the trail (eye 1.7 m over the terrain, 6.5 m/s, head bob)\n402\tbetween vantages, 4-8 s, skippable.\n403\t\n404\t**Tests** (unit/lens): project then ray returns the same point; horizon row matches pitch; magnified lens centre ray\n405\tequals the main ray at `s0`; director keyframes reproduce the same yaw at the same time across two runs.\n406\t\n407\t### 5.2 Terrain (`vista.js`: `E.Heightfield`)\n408\t\n409\tEach hunt is one landscape: a **core** of 1024 x 1024 m at 1 m height samples (Float32, 4 MB) and a **far ring** of\n410\t4096 x 4096 m at 8 m samples (the same noise, no features) for distant hills. A 1 m RGBA colour map covers the core\n411\t(4 MB); a ground-type map (Uint8) picks a 64 x 64 micro-texture for near pixels. Generation (boot step or a loading\n412\tcard, about 1 s per hunt; headless runs skip the colour bake):\n413\t\n414\t1. **Base**: fBm of `E.noise2` (5 octaves, lacunarity 2, gain 0.5) with domain warping; amplitude and scale per hunt.\n415\t2. **Features**, in order, from the hunt's data (coordinates in metres in the core):\n416\t   - `ridge { path, height, width }`: a raised Gaussian profile along a Catmull-Rom spline (`E.Path`).\n417\t   - `river { path, width, depth, banks }`: carves a channel with sloped banks; marks water.\n418\t   - `lake { center, radii, depth, level }`: a bowl; marks water at `level`.\n419\t   - `flatten { poly, z: number | 'avg', falloff }`: meadows, fields, the farmyard.\n420\t   - `field { poly, crop: 'corn', rowsDeg }`: ground type and row texture; corn-row props scattered by rule.\n421\t   - `trail { path, width }`: slight flattening, a dirt ground type; the walk-in paths follow trails.\n422\t   - `clearView { vantage, bearings: [a, b], dist: [near, far] }`: lowers any terrain between the vantage's eye and\n423\t     the fan's far edge so nothing unplanned blocks the play area (authored hills in the fan stay, under a margin).\n424\t   - `snow { above, amount }`, `burn { poly }`: ground types.\n425\t3. **Water**: level and mask; a lake or river surface is drawn at its level wherever the ground is below it.\n426\t4. **Ground types** by rules (slope, height, distance to water, masks, noise): grass, dry grass, leaf litter, dirt,\n427\t   rock, talus, mud, reeds, snow, ice, corn, burn. Each has a macro colour and a micro tile.\n428\t5. **Light** (bake): normals from central differences; Lambert with the hunt's sun direction and colours; a cheap\n429\t   ambient occlusion (height minus a blurred height); sun shadows by marching toward the sun in 2 m steps; tree shadows\n430\t   stamped as soft ellipses offset along the sun direction after scatter. Dithered into the colour map.\n431\t6. **Scatter** (`E.gen.scatter` with the `world` seed): forests, groves and single trees, bushes, rocks, logs, stumps,\n432\t   reeds, tall grass, corn rows, snowdrifts, by mask rules per hunt; never inside a site's play fan except authored\n433\t   cover. Each placed prop registers its occluder volumes in a 32 m spatial grid (5.9).\n434\t\n435\tSampling: `height(x, y)` bilinear (core, else far ring); `color(x, y)`; `type(x, y)`; `normal(x, y)`;\n436\t`water(x, y)` gives the surface level or null; `rayHit(origin, dir, maxD)` marches 0.5 m steps (growing with distance)\n437\tthen bisects.\n438\t\n439\t**Tests** (unit/terrain): a hunt's generation hash is stable for its seed; a river's bed is at least its depth below\n440\tthe banks; lake water level and mask agree; `clearView` leaves the fan's sightline clear (rays from the eye to 50\n441\tsample points in the fan reach their target); `rayHit` agrees with brute-force sampling within 0.1 m.\n442\t\n443\t### 5.3 The renderer (`vista.js`: `E.Vista`)\n444\t\n445\t`vista.draw(r, lens, opts)` draws a full frame into the game buffer as one `r.overlay(g => ...)`. `vista.render(g, lens,\n446\trect)` is the same pass into any rectangle (the scope lens, the Trophy Cam, the title).\n447\t\n448\t1. **Sky and Backdrop.** An `E.Backdrop` per hunt (sky gradient, stars, cloud and fog layers, far mountain layers)\n449\t   drawn above the horizon through a stand-in renderer whose `ix = yaw * f * 10` so it turns with the camera (as\n450\t   `mode7.js drawSky` does). Our own sun or moon disc with a dithered glow is placed by its world bearing and elevation\n451\t   (not the Backdrop's screen-fixed sun). Dusk adds dithered additive god rays.\n452\t2. **Terrain raster** (front to back, one column at a time, into a `Uint32Array` frame with alpha 0 for sky, then\n453\t   `putImageData` into a scratch canvas drawn over the sky):\n454\t   - for column `sx`: ground direction `F + R*((sx - cx)/f)`; march D from 1 m with step `max(0.15 m, D*0.0075)` to\n455\t     the core's edge, then the far ring with coarser steps, to the hunt's far distance (about 3 km), stopping when\n456\t     the column is full;\n457\t   - at each step, sample height `h`; `y = horizonY - (h - C.z)*f/D`; if `y` is above the column's current top (the\n458\t     column fills from the bottom of the screen upward), fill from `y` down to that top with the ground colour, shaded\n459\t     by distance fog (`fogNear..fogFar`) and height fog (valley mist below `mistTop`), and for near spans (D < 25 m)\n460\t     with per-pixel micro-texture (the pixel's ground point found on the plane of that sample) and ordered dither;\n461\t     the column's top becomes `y`;\n462\t   - record an **occlusion breakpoint** `(D, y)` each time the column's top changes (typed arrays, up to 512 per\n463\t     column): `occY(col, D)` is the topmost terrain pixel drawn by samples nearer than D;\n464\t   - water: where the ground is under the water level, draw the surface at the level: the sky colour at the mirrored\n465\t     row, darkened and tinted, with animated ripple bands and sun glints; shoreline foam; ice edges in Muskeg.\n466\t3. **Billboards**, gathered each frame, sorted far to near, each `{ x, y, z, D, k, bounds: [w, h], draw }`:\n467\t   - skip if off screen or past the fog's far edge; compute its screen box from its world bounds;\n468\t   - if `occY(col, D - 0.6 m)` is below the box's bottom for every column, draw straight onto the frame;\n469\t     if it is above the box's top everywhere, skip it; otherwise draw it into a screen-sized scratch canvas, clear\n470\t     each column below `occY` (`clearRect(x, occY, 1, ...)`), and draw the box onto the frame. The 0.6 m bias keeps\n471\t     hooves from being clipped by the ground they stand on;\n472\t   - fog: billboards past `fogNear` are drawn through a fog tint (pre-tinted impostor levels, or `px.blend` with the\n473\t     fog colour at stepped alpha).\n474\t4. **Impostors** (`E.Impostors`): props are drawn from sprites pre-rendered at 24 scale levels (k from 0.01 to 3\n475\t   pixels per unit, geometric) and up to 8 facings, picked at the nearest level at or below the needed k and drawn\n476\t   with nearest-neighbour `drawImage` scaling of at most 1.12x (`E.ctx2d` contexts: smoothing off). Library shapes\n477\t   come from `E.shapeSprite(id, { view, facing, size })` with a Vista view at that scale; custom props from our\n478\t   painters `paint(g, k, variant)`. Levels are built lazily and pre-warmed in boot steps for the current hunt. Near\n479\t   trees (D < 40 m) sway: their top rows are drawn shifted by a sine of time (wind).\n480\t5. **Animals and critters** are drawn live each frame: `rig.draw(g, sx, sy, view)` with the cached view for their k\n481\t   and pitch, plus a 1-pixel outline (draw the rig's silhouette offset 4 ways in the outline colour into the scratch\n482\t   canvas, then the rig). The outline colour is per hunt (warm orange at dusk reads as rim light). LOD: below 1.2\n483\t   pixels per unit skip ear, tail and eye details; below 6 pixels tall draw a 3-tone silhouette (but always draw\n484\t   antlers, 5.4).\n485\t6. **World particles** (`12-fx.js`): dust, dirt, bark chips, leaves, water droplets, snow puffs, glass and clay shards,\n486\t   feathers, breath vapour; projected each frame, drawn as `px.rect`/`px.disc` sized by k (at least 1 pixel). Cap 1500.\n487\t7. **Foreground dressing** per vantage, in screen space with slight parallax: tree-stand rail, blind window edges,\n488\t   canoe bow, grass tufts and leaves at the bottom corners. Plus near grass-tuft billboards 3-8 m out that hide\n489\t   hooves.\n490\t8. **Weather and grade**: screen-space snow, leaves or drizzle in three parallax layers (cosmetic RNG); the hunt's\n491\t   colour grade; a light vignette.\n492\t9. **Scope lenses**: for each scoped player, render the world again into a `2R x 2R` scratch canvas with the\n493\t   magnified lens (5.1) about that player's swayed aim point, mask it to a circle (a `px.disc` mask with\n494\t   `destination-in`), draw it at the reticle, then the scope ring, crosshair and breath arc. R = 54 px at 480 x 270.\n495\t\n496\tThe renderer must also be callable with a flat terrain (all heights 0, `opts.flat`) for milestone M1 and as the\n497\tfallback in section 8.\n498\t\n499\t**Tests**: unit/vista (headless): occlusion breakpoints are monotonic per column; `occY` matches a brute-force\n500\treference for random columns; billboard sort is far to near. browser/vista: no errors; screenshots of each hunt's\n501\tthree vantages at fixed seeds go in `tests/out/` for review; a pick-agreement check (5.9).\n502\t\n503\t### 5.4 Four-legged rigs (`quadruped.js`: `E.Quadruped`)\n504\t\n505\tBuilt like the engine's Humanoid: a 3D skeleton posed by math every step from continuous inputs, drawn as shaded pixel\n506\tvolumes through any `E.View`. API:\n507\t\n508\t```js\n509\tconst rig = new E.Quadruped({ species: 'whitetail', sex: 'buck', size: 1, colors: {...}, antlers: { points: 8, seed: 3 } });\n510\trig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, ground: (x, y) => h, gait: 'auto', alert: 0..1, graze: 0..1, look: { x, y, z },\n511\t                 hurt: 0..1, limp: legIndex | -1, jump: 0..1, dead: 0..1, call: 0..1 /* bugle or grunt pose */, splash });\n512\trig.draw(g, ox, oy, view);            // inside a billboard\n513\trig.J                                 // joints in world space after update (headless too)\n514\trig.zones()                           // hit volumes in world space (5.9)\n515\trig.drawXray(g, x, y, scale, hits)    // side-on x-ray for the Shot Chart\n516\trig.drawMount(g, x, y, size)          // head-and-antlers plaque for the Lodge and HUD icons\n517\trig.debug(g, view)                    // skeleton overlay for dev mode\n518\t```\n519\t\n520\t**Skeleton** (local frame: x forward, y right, z up, units = world units at `size: 1` for a whitetail; other species\n521\tscale it): pelvis, mid-spine, withers, neck base, neck mid, head, muzzle, ears L/R, tail base and tip; four legs, each\n522\ttop joint (shoulder or hip), elbow or stifle, knee or hock, fetlock, hoof.\n523\t\n524\t| Whitetail rest pose (units; 16 = 1 m) | |\n525\t|---|---|\n526\t| Spine: pelvis -> withers | (-10, 0, 14.5) -> (8, 0, 15.8) (shoulder height 1.0 m) |\n527\t| Torso cross-sections (at x: top z, bottom z, half width) | chest x 5: 15.8, 8.8, 3.2; belly x -2: 14.8, 9.6, 3.4; rump x -9: 14.6, 10.4, 3.0 |\n528\t| Fore leg top (shoulder joint) / hind leg top (hip joint) | (8, +-2.4, 10.8) / (-10, +-2.6, 12) |\n529\t| Fore bones: humerus, forearm, cannon, pastern+hoof | 3.8, 4.6, 3.8, 1.4 (a gentle zigzag at rest) |\n530\t| Hind bones: femur, tibia, cannon, pastern+hoof | 4.6, 5.2, 4.4, 1.4 (stifle forward, hock back) |\n531\t| Neck base -> head | (9, 0, 15.5) -> (14.5, 0, 22.5), about 50 degrees up; head 5.5 long, muzzle tipped 25 degrees down |\n532\t| Ears, tail | ears 2.8 long at the back of the skull; tail base (-11.5, 0, 14.6), 3.5 long |\n533\t\n534\tElk: x1.5 with a longer neck, dark neck mane, pale rump patch. Moose: x1.9, long legs (legs x1.15 relative), humped\n535\twithers, long heavy head with a dewlap (\"bell\"), short tail. Fawn and calf: x0.6 with big ears, spots on fawns.\n536\tCanid preset (fox, coyote, wolf): digitigrade legs, bushy tail, pointed ears. Cow preset (Bessie): bulky, black and\n537\twhite patches, a bell.\n538\t\n539\t**Gaits** (per-leg phase offsets [LF, RF, LH, RH] and stance share of the cycle):\n540\t\n541\t| Gait | Offsets | Stance | Notes |\n542\t|---|---|---|---|\n543\t| walk | 0.25, 0.75, 0, 0.5 | 0.65 | 4-beat; head nods with each fore step |\n544\t| trot | 0, 0.5, 0.5, 0 | 0.45 | diagonal pairs; small body bob |\n545\t| gallop | 0.5, 0.6, 0, 0.1 | 0.30 | spine flexes +-5%, body pitches, a flight phase |\n546\t| bound | 0.45, 0.5, 0, 0.05 | 0.25 | the whitetail escape: big arcs, tail flagged up showing white |\n547\t\n548\tStride length grows with speed; the cycle frequency is speed / stride. `gait: 'auto'` picks by speed with blending.\n549\tHooves in stance are locked to the ground point where they touched down (no sliding); in swing they arc from lift-off\n550\tto the predicted next touchdown (body position + velocity x the remaining swing and half a stance). Leg solve: the\n551\tfetlock target sits above the hoof; the cannon bone's angle comes from the phase (upright in stance, folded in\n552\tswing: fore cannons fold back, hind cannons angle forward); then `E.ik3` from the top joint to the knee or hock with\n553\tthe bend hint (fore elbows bend back, hind stifles bend forward). Body pitch and roll follow the ground under the four\n554\thooves (`ground(x, y)`); the heading turns toward the velocity with a limit, leaning into turns.\n555\t\n556\t**Poses** (continuous weights, blended): idle (breathing, weight shifts, ear flicks, tail swish), graze (neck to the\n557\tground, jaw chewing), alert (head high, ears forward toward `look`, a foreleg stomp now and then), look-at (head and\n558\tneck turn within limits), startle (crouch, then spring), jump (legs tucked over a log, fence or creek), call (elk:\n559\tneck stretched and mouth open while the bugle sounds; moose: head low, grunting), hurt (a flinch: body roll, head\n560\ttoss, 0.25 s), limp (one leg's stride cut to 40%), death (1.0 s: front knees buckle, chest drops, hind legs give, the\n561\tbody rolls about 70 degrees onto its side away from the shot, the head settles last), dead (lying, still).\n562\t\n563\t**Antlers** (`E.Antlers`): generated 3D polylines from a seed, drawn after the head with 1-3 pixel thickness by k,\n564\tbone-coloured with darker bases and a dark outline:\n565\t- whitetail: main beams sweep back, out and forward; tines rise from the beam; brow tines; `points` 4-12 (typical);\n566\t  the trophy has 14 with kickers and a drop tine;\n567\t- elk: long beams sweeping back, 5-7 tines per side, a long dagger fourth tine (the trophy is 7 x 7);\n568\t- moose: palmate paddles with points along the rim, spread 40-55 inches (the trophy 72 inches).\n569\tReadability: every animal is drawn (and zoned) at `animalScale` 1.1, an arcade size boost (dev slider); antlers are\n570\tdrawn at x1.15 more (dev slider) and grow with distance, x(1 + max(0, (D - 80 m) / 150 m)) capped at 1.6, so bucks\n571\tread at range; an optional assist setting adds a one-pixel glint on antler tips.\n572\t\n573\t**Tests** (unit/quadruped): every joint is finite through each gait at several speeds; planted hooves move less than\n574\t0.5 units during stance; the death pose ends with the pelvis below 40% of standing height; zones follow joints\n575\t(the heart sphere stays inside the chest); antler generation is deterministic per seed and has the requested points.\n576\tVisual: `tools/filmstrip.mjs` strips of walk, trot, gallop, bound, graze-to-alert, death for each species, reviewed by\n577\teye (the one thing a script can't judge), from the Animal Lab deep link.\n578\t\n579\t### 5.5 Critters, people and other rigs\n580\t- **Small critters** use the engine's `E.Blob` (feet, ears, tail, wings, mouth options) plus small extra drawing (a\n581\t  raccoon's mask and ringed tail, a skunk's stripe, a beaver's flat tail, a turkey's fan and wattle).\n582\t- **Birds** (crows, grouse, ducks, ravens, eagle, loons): Blob with wings and `flap`; flight on arcs and `E.Path`s.\n583\t- **Canids and the cow** use `E.Quadruped` presets.\n584\t- **People** are `E.Humanoid`s (size 1.3): Uncle Dub (bulky, blaze-orange cap, canvas coat, grey beard), Granny Mae\n585\t  (chibi, grey bun, shawl, rolling pin), Cousin Dwayne (heroic, mirrored shades, camo), Loretta Pruitt (heroic,\n586\t  ponytail, red flannel), Big Earl (bulky, overalls, chili pot), the Emcee (cap, bow tie, microphone). They appear as\n587\t  dialog portraits (`talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`), in the Lodge, and in-world in bonus games (Granny on\n588\t  her porch; Loretta at the trap house; Earl by the lake fire). Use their engine poses: `wave`, `cheer`, `hips`\n589\t  (Dwayne's taunt), `throw` (Granny's crow-scaring rolling pin wave), `kneel`.\n590\t- **Snowmen** (bonus): stacked shaded discs with stick arms and hats, a throw animation, a topple.\n591\t\n592\t### 5.6 Animal AI (`30-actors.js`, `31-patterns.js`)\n593\t\n594\tEach animal is an actor `{ id, species, sex, rig, x, y, z, vx, vy, heading, state, orders[], hp, fear, wounded,\n595\tbagger, ... }`. States: `hidden` (behind cover, not yet out), `enter`, `walk`, `trot`, `run`, `graze`, `look`, `stop`,\n596\t`jump`, `wade`, `wounded`, `dying`, `dead`, `exit`, `gone`.\n597\t\n598\t- **Orders** come from spawn patterns, as a queue: `['walk', { to: 0.4 }]`, `['graze', 3]`, `['look', 1.5]`,\n599\t  `['trot', { to: 0.8 }]`, `['run', { to: 1 }]`, `['exit']`. Movement follows the actor's path (an `E.Path` of\n600\t  terrain points; z from the heightfield); path points can carry `jump` marks (logs, fences, the creek) where the rig\n601\t  leaps.\n602\t- **Herds**: followers trail their leader's path at `[back, side]` offsets with 0.1-0.4 s reaction delays (from the\n603\t  `ai` stream) and keep 1.5 m apart.\n604\t- **Senses and spooking**: a gunshot emits `noise` at the shooter (loudness 1, heard to 400 m) and at the impact\n605\t  (0.6 within 40 m). Each animal adds fear by loudness and distance; past its threshold it startles (alert 0.15-0.45 s),\n606\t  then flees along its path (or the pattern's escape route) at run speed. A near miss (impact within 4 m) bolts it at\n607\t  once with a jump. Fleeing animals that hear more shots speed up 10%. Species temper: whitetail skittish (threshold\n608\t  0.35), elk herd-minded (the herd flees together), moose stubborn (threshold 0.7; may stop and stare instead).\n609\t- **Calls** (5.8): within the call's radius, animals that are not panicking stop and look toward the caller for\n610\t  `callFreeze` seconds (1.4 +- 0.2), then go on (moose may take a few steps toward you). Each animal answers a call at\n611\t  most twice per site. Bull elk may bugle back. Trophies have their own rules (6.3).\n612\t- **Wounds and death**: zone damage (5.9) reduces hp; at 0 it dies. A wounded animal limps (speed x0.6, the hit\n613\t  leg's stride cut) and heads for the nearest exit; further hits take more hp; if it leaves the frame it is **LOST**\n614\t  (a penalty). A killed\n615\t  animal plays `dying` then lies `dead` with a tag over it (the bagger's colour, its points) until the site ends.\n616\t- **Exits**: an animal past its path's end or outside the camera's bearing range plus 10 degrees is `gone`; a buck\n617\t  that goes is **ESCAPED** (its HUD icon is crossed out).\n618\t- **Visibility** (for quick-shot bonuses and the bot): on screen, and an analytic ray from the camera to its chest is\n619\t  clear of terrain and occluders.\n620\t\n621\t**Spawn patterns** (data; each places one or more actors with paths in site-relative `[bearing, dist]` points and\n622\torder queues; variations from the `spawn` stream):\n623\t\n624\t| Pattern | What it does |\n625\t|---|---|\n626\t| `cross(path, gait)` | enters at the path's start, crosses, exits |\n627\t| `emerge(cover, path)` | waits hidden behind a cover prop, steps out alert, then goes on |\n628\t| `graze(point, secs)` | walks in, grazes with glances up, wanders off |\n629\t| `herdPass(path, n, spacing)` | a leader and followers on one path |\n630\t| `ridgeRun(path)` | a fast run along a crest, silhouetted against the sky |\n631\t| `wade(path)` | moves through water: slow, splashing, legs hidden below the surface |\n632\t| `bolt(bed, dir)` | bedded down unseen; jumps up and runs at the first shot or after a time |\n633\t| `decoyDoe(buck)` | a doe timed to cross in front of a buck (patience test) |\n634\t| `stand(point, secs)` | stops broadside in the open (an easy shot, used sparingly) |\n635\t| `charge(target)` | runs straight at the vantage (Big Mo, the timberline bull) |\n636\t| `fight(a, b, secs)` | two bulls lock antlers, then split |\n637\t\n638\t**Tests** (unit/ai): a shot within hearing makes calm animals flee within 0.5 s; a near miss bolts at once; calls\n639\tfreeze non-panicking animals for 1.2-1.6 s and never more than twice each; followers keep spacing; a wounded buck that\n640\texits counts as lost; a killed buck stays dead and tagged; every pattern runs to `gone` or `dead` without errors.\n641\t\n642\t### 5.7 Sites (`32-site.js`)\n643\t\n644\tA site is data plus a script:\n645\t\n646\t```js\n647\tdef('site', 'hollow-1', {\n648\t  hunt: 'hollow', name: 'Creek Crossing', vantage: 'creekStand',       // the hunt's vantage: x, y (m), eye height, base yaw\n649\t  frame: { bearings: [-38, 38], fov: 34, pitch: 3 },                  // the play fan; camera limits\n650\t  director: [[0, { bearing: -12 }], [6, { bearing: 4, dur: 8, ease: 'inOutSine' }], [30, { mode: 'follow' }]],\n651\t  time: 60, bucks: 3,                                                 // bucks scheduled (the HUD shows them)\n652\t  props: [['log', [-20, 32]], ['bush', [14, 28]]],                     // authored cover, [bearing, dist]\n653\t  script: function* (S) {                                             // a game.script coroutine; S = the site API\n654\t    yield 1.2; S.bark('start');\n655\t    S.pattern('cross', { kind: 'doe', path: [[-35, 44], [-5, 36], [30, 46]], gait: 'walk' });\n656\t    S.pattern('cross', { kind: 'fawn', follow: S.last, back: 2.5 });\n657\t    yield 5;\n658\t    S.pattern('graze', { kind: 'buck', points: S.rng.int(5, 8), at: [10, 34], secs: 4 });\n659\t    yield E.any([E.until(() => S.bucksLeft <= 2), 14]);\n660\t    S.pattern('herdPass', { kinds: ['buck', 'doe', 'buck'], path: [[38, 28], [0, 24], [-40, 40]], gait: 'bound', splash: true });\n661\t    yield E.until(() => S.allResolved);                               // every buck spawned so far bagged, escaped or lost\n662\t  },\n663\t  critters: [['squirrel', { tree: [-30, 22], at: 8 }], ['rabbit', { path: [[20, 15], [5, 12]], at: 20 }]]\n664\t});\n665\t```\n666\t\n667\t- `S` gives: `pattern`, `spawn`, `bark`, `say` (captions), `rng` (with `int`, `pick`, `chance`), `time`, `bucksLeft`,\n668\t  `actors`, `players`, `on(event, fn)` (bus events such as calls and shots, cleared when the site ends),\n669\t  `allResolved`, `last` (the last actor spawned).\n670\t- **End rules**: the site ends when every scheduled buck is bagged, escaped or lost and the script has finished, or\n671\t  when time runs out (TIME!: the rest flee), or when every player is benched. Then `siteEnd` (1.5 s of the last\n672\t  animals running off), then the tally.\n673\t- In AI Hub rounds and Party Quick Draw, sites are **time-boxed**: they run their full time and their scripts loop\n674\t  their waves; bucks keep coming.\n675\t- Results per player: shots, hits, kills by zone, distances, bonuses, penalties, stars, the Shot Chart records.\n676\t\n677\t**Tests** (flow/sites): for each site at seeds 1-3: the bot (cheat aim) bags every buck and the site ends early with a\n678\tclean sweep; with no shots the site ends on time with every buck escaped; shooting the first doe benches the player\n679\tand (single player) ends the site; the bucks scheduled equal the bucks spawned; no errors.\n680\t\n681\t### 5.8 Rifle, scope and call (`40-weapon.js`) and aiming (`41-aim.js`)\n682\t\n683\t**Rifle** (each player): `rounds` left (5 when full; dev slider 1-10) and a `chambered` flag. A site starts with 5\n684\trounds and one chambered. The HUD shows 5 shell icons (rounds) and a chamber light.\n685\t- Fire: needs a chambered round and 0.1 s since the last shot. It spends a round and empties the chamber.\n686\t  Fire with an empty chamber: a dry click, the reticle shakes, and the PUMP prompt flashes (a bark the first two times\n687\t  per hunt).\n688\t- Pump: with rounds left, racks in 0.22 s (two clicks) and chambers the next one. With none left, the pump reloads:\n689\t  1.25 s, five shell clicks, 5 rounds and one chambered. So a tube is 5 shots. Pumping while racking or reloading does\n690\t  nothing.\n691\t- **Auto-pump** setting (off by default; Easy difficulty and the attract bot turn it on) racks 0.12 s after each shot.\n692\t- Bonus games use their own tube (10-12) and a faster rack (0.15 s).\n693\t\n694\t**Scope** (hold, or toggle by setting): raises in 0.15 s; draws the lens (5.3 step 9) at x3.5 magnification (dev\n695\tslider 1.5-6); pad reticle speed x0.45 while scoped. **Sway**: the aim point drifts by\n696\t`A * (0.6 sin 1.3t + 0.4 sin(2.9t+1), 0.5 sin(1.7t+2) + 0.3 sin 3.3t)` screen pixels, `A = 2.6` (slider). **Hold\n697\tbreath** (held): sway x0.15 for up to 3.0 s (slider), the meter refills at 1.2 per second after letting go; emptying it\n698\tforces an exhale and doubles sway for 1.5 s. The lens view drifts with the sway; its crosshair stays centred; the shot\n699\tgoes through the swayed point.\n700\t\n701\t**Call** (species-specific sound: whitetail grunt, elk bugle, moose grunt): 2 charges per player per site (slider\n702\t0-5), 3 s cooldown, radius 200 m, effect in 5.6. A call is a shared event: in multiplayer, everyone's animals stop.\n703\t\n704\t**Reticle** (each player, in screen pixels of the 480 x 270 buffer):\n705\t- Mouse: the reticle is the pointer (`input.pointer()`), absolute; keeps its place when the pointer leaves.\n706\t- Stick: `input.move()` (left stick, d-pad, and for keyboard-only players WASD and arrows) gives a velocity: max 400\n707\t  px/s (slider), response curve `|v|^1.7`, 0.15 s ramp to full speed; the right stick (`input.padAim`) adds fine aim at\n708\t  x0.3; scoped x0.45.\n709\t- **Aim assist** (pads and keyboard only, never the mouse; setting Off / Light / Strong; default Light): friction x0.65\n710\t  (Light) or x0.45 (Strong) when the reticle is within 8 px of a live target's projected hit volume; Strong also pulls\n711\t  25% of the way toward the vital zone per second while the stick moves toward it. Never toward does or protected\n712\t  critters.\n713\t- Each player's reticle has its own shape as well as colour: P1 circle-cross, P2 diamond, P3 square brackets, P4\n714\t  triangle. Benched players' reticles turn grey with a BENCHED tag.\n715\t\n716\t**Tests** (unit/weapon): fire, pump and reload timings at 1/120 s steps; dry fire with an empty chamber; auto-pump;\n717\treload refills the tube; sway is deterministic for a time and shrinks while holding breath; breath empties after 3.0\n718\ts and forces the shaky phase; calls spend charges and respect the cooldown. (unit/aim): stick curve and ramp; mouse\n719\tabsolute; assist friction applies to bucks and not to does; scoped speed scale.\n720\t\n721\t### 5.9 Shot resolution and hit zones (`42-shot.js`)\n722\t\n723\tA shot is analytic (it works headless and matches what is drawn): the player's ray (5.1) at the swayed aim point.\n724\tCandidates, nearest forward distance wins:\n725\t1. **Animals**: bounding sphere first; then every zone the ray crosses on that animal (spheres and capsules from\n726\t   `rig.zones()`); the scoring zone is the highest-priority zone crossed on the first animal hit: heart > head >\n727\t   lung > neck > shoulder > gut > ham > leg > antler. A doe or fawn in front of a buck takes the shot.\n728\t2. **Critters**: spheres (and a capsule for long ones).\n729\t3. **Occluders**: tree trunks (vertical cylinders), rocks (spheres), logs (capsules), fence posts, hay bales, the barn,\n730\t   stand and blind frames (boxes); looked up along the ray's ground track in the 32 m grid. Foliage, bushes, reeds,\n731\t   grass and corn do not block shots (they only hide).\n732\t4. **Terrain**: `rayHit` up to the nearest candidate's distance.\n733\t5. **Water**: the surface plane where the mask says water.\n734\t\n735\t| Zone (whitetail at rest, local units) | Volume | Damage | Lethal | Score x | Callout |\n736\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n737\t| heart | sphere r 1.5 at (6, 0, 10.6): low in the chest, just behind the fore leg | 9 | yes | 2.0 | HEART SHOT! |\n738\t| head | sphere r 2.0 at the skull | 9 | yes | 1.8 | HEADSHOT! |\n739\t| lung | ellipsoid radii (3.6, 2.7, 2.6) at (4.5, 0, 12.2) | 9 | yes | 1.6 | LUNG SHOT! |\n740\t| neck | capsule neck base to head base, r 1.8 | 9 | yes | 1.4 | NECK SHOT! |\n741\t| shoulder | sphere r 2.2 at each fore leg top | 9 | yes | 1.2 | SHOULDER! |\n742\t| gut | capsule (3, 0, 12.2) to (-8, 0, 12.4), r 3.1 | 0.6 | no | 0.7 | GUT SHOT |\n743\t| ham | sphere r 3.0 at (-9, 0, 12.4) | 0.5 | no | 0.6 | HAM HIT |\n744\t| leg | a capsule per bone, r 1.1 (upper bones) and 0.8 (lower) | 0.4 | no | 0.5 | LEG HIT |\n745\t| antler | capsules along beams and tines, r 0.35 | 0 | no | 0 | ANTLER PING! (no damage; spooks) |\n746\t\n747\tZones are rebuilt from the joints every step (they bend, run and fall with the rig) and every zone stays inside the\n748\tdrawn body, so a shot just off the silhouette misses.\n749\t\n750\tHit points: whitetail 1, elk 1.5, moose 2 (a lethal zone kills any of them; a deer drops to two gut shots, a moose to\n751\tfour). Elk and moose zones scale with the rig.\n752\tEffects by what was hit: fur puff and thump (animal), feathers (bird), bark chips and a wood knock (trunk), sparks and a\n753\tricochet whine (rock), dirt clods and a thud (terrain), a splash column (water), nothing but the echo (sky). Every shot\n754\temits spook noise (5.6). Same-step hits on one animal by two players: the better zone gets it; equal zones share the\n755\tkill (SHARED! half points each).\n756\t\n757\tHits are stored in the animal's local frame (position, heading, body pitch and roll at the moment) for the Shot Chart.\n758\t\n759\t**Tests** (unit/shot): rays through each zone's centre on a posed whitetail, elk and moose return that zone; a ray\n760\tthrough the heart from the side passes the lung first and still scores heart; a trunk in front blocks; a crest in\n761\tfront blocks; a doe in front of a buck takes the shot; antler ping does no damage; water plane splash; equal-zone\n762\tsame-step hits share. **browser/pick** (the WYSIWYG check): render each live animal of a busy scene as a flat ID\n763\tcolour into an offscreen canvas, sample 300 pixels inside the silhouettes (not antlers) and cast analytic rays: at\n764\tleast 95% must hit the animal drawn there; sample 300 pixels 3 px outside the silhouettes: at most 5% may hit it.\n765\t\n766\t### 5.10 Scoring (`43-score.js`)\n767\t\n768\t```\n769\tkill points = round( base(species, antlers) x distanceMult x zoneMult x (running ? 1.25 : 1) x (trophy ? 3 : 1) ) + quick + multi\n770\trunning = the animal was moving faster than its species' trot speed when hit; zoneMult = the killing shot's zone\n771\tbase: whitetail 100 + 15 per point; elk 150 + 20 per point; moose 200 + 6 per inch of spread\n772\tdistanceMult = clamp(1 + (metres - 25) / 75, 1, 3.5)          (100 m = 2.0; 175 m = 3.0)\n773\tquick = +50 if the kill comes within 1.0 s of the buck first being visible\n774\tmulti = DOUBLE! +250 for a second kill within 1.5 s, TRIPLE! +750 for a third\n775\t```\n776\t\n777\tSite bonuses: **Accuracy** `round(hits / shots x 1000)` when at least 50% (hits on any target count; antler pings\n778\tdon't); **Clean sweep** +1000 (every buck bagged, every kill one shot in a lethal zone); **No penalties** +200.\n779\tCritters: fixed points (6.7). Penalties: doe or fawn: benched for the site (Classic) or -300 (Casual); skunk -100 and a\n780\tgreen stink cloud over the screen for 3 s; Bessie -500; eagle or loon -400; a lost wounded animal -100. Scores never go\n781\tbelow 0 per site.\n782\t\n783\t**Stars** per site (antlers, 1-3): 1 for bagging a buck, 2 for bagging every buck, 3 for a clean sweep with no\n784\tpenalties. **Rank** after a hunt, against its par (tuned to the bot's average): Greenhorn < 40%, Tenderfoot < 60%,\n785\tWoodsman < 80%, Sharpshooter < 100%, Big Game Pro < 125%, Legend of the Lodge from 125%.\n786\t\n787\t**Rivals** (Hunt-Off campaign): Big Earl, Loretta Pruitt and Cousin Dwayne post preset scores per hunt (start at 55%,\n788\t85% and 100% of par; tune). The HUD shows Dwayne's score for the hunt as the one to beat; beating him gives +1000\n789\tBRAGGING RIGHTS and a grumble.\n790\t\n791\t**Tests** (unit/score): the formula for given inputs (table-driven); multi-kill windows; accuracy threshold; clean\n792\tsweep only with one-shot lethal kills; penalties per setting; stars; ranks at the boundaries; scores never negative.\n793\t\n794\t### 5.11 Players and local multiplayer (`50-players.js`)\n795\t- `game.players` (engine): P1 is `game.input` (mouse and keyboard, or a pad); players 2-4 join with Options/Start on a\n796\t  free pad (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`) on the Party Hunt join page; a second keyboard half\n797\t  (`keys: 'right'`, arrows to aim) is allowed but not advertised.\n798\t- Colours are the AI Hub seat colours: P1 `#e8443a` red, P2 `#3b82f6` blue, P3 `#22c55e` green, P4 `#f5b700` gold,\n799\t  plus the reticle shapes (5.8) so colour is never the only cue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3fa61e8-1431-4900-ae89-edc59162574b","uuid":"d0979bea-d51f-407d-a399-657b378d5e86","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:06:04.894Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050554__x4cdb93/work/PLAN.md","content":"**Projection** (a sheared perspective: verticals stay vertical, which suits billboards and the column renderer):\n- Camera `{ x, y, z, yaw, pitch, fov }` (fov = degrees across the screen's height; pitch + = looking down).\n- Screen `W x H`, centre `cx = W/2`, `cy = H/2`; focal `f = (H/2) / tan(fov/2)`; `horizonY = cy - f * tan(pitch)`.\n- Forward `F = (cos yaw, sin yaw)`, right `R = (-sin yaw, cos yaw)`.\n- `project(P)`: `d = P - C`; `D = d.F` (forward distance); `L = d.R`; null if `D < 1 m`; `k = f / D` (pixels per unit);\n  `sx = cx + L*k`; `sy = horizonY - (P.z - C.z)*k`. Returns `[sx, sy, k, D]`.\n- `ray(sx, sy)`: direction `dir = F + R*((sx - cx)/f) + up*((horizonY - sy)/f)`; the point at forward distance D is\n  `C + D*dir`. Shots, picking and the bot all use this.\n- **Magnifying about a point** (the scope lens): the same camera with focal `f*mag`, centred so screen point `s0` lands\n  on the lens centre: `s' = c_lens + (s - s0)*mag`. The lens is an exact zoom of the main view, so the shot through its\n  centre is the main view's ray through `s0`.\n- **Rigs and props** are drawn as orthographic billboards: an `E.View('vista', 'Vista', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)` with\n  `yawDeg = -yaw*180/pi - 90` (Mode 7's formula) and `pitchDeg` = the angle from the camera down to the object,\n  clamped to 4..30 degrees, quantized to 2 degrees; views cached by (k in 1/32 steps below 1, 1/16 above; yaw in 2\n  degree steps; pitch).\n\n**Director** (per site, deterministic; keyed to site time, never to player input, so all players and all AI Hub copies\nsee the same frame): keyframes `[t, { bearing, pitch, fov, ease, dur }]`; optional `follow` mode in single player\n(yaw eases toward the centroid of live bucks, clamped to the site's bearing range, smoothed over 0.8 s). Single player\nalso gets a 0.4 degree recoil kick per shot (recovers in 0.25 s) and a slow breathing drift (0.15 degrees); local\nmultiplayer and hub rounds use keyframes only and no kick. Canoe vantage: a gentle bob (z +-4 cm, roll +-0.6 degrees,\nperiod 3.1 s). Walk-ins: the camera follows an `E.Path` along the trail (eye 1.7 m over the terrain, 6.5 m/s, head bob)\nbetween vantages, 4-8 s, skippable.\n\n**Tests** (unit/lens): project then ray returns the same point; horizon row matches pitch; magnified lens centre ray\nequals the main ray at `s0`; director keyframes reproduce the same yaw at the same time across two runs.\n\n### 5.2 Terrain (`vista.js`: `E.Heightfield`)\n\nEach hunt is one landscape: a **core** of 1024 x 1024 m at 1 m height samples (Float32, 4 MB) and a **far ring** of\n4096 x 4096 m at 8 m samples (the same noise, no features) for distant hills. A 1 m RGBA colour map covers the core\n(4 MB); a ground-type map (Uint8) picks a 64 x 64 micro-texture for near pixels. Generation (boot step or a loading\ncard, about 1 s per hunt; headless runs skip the colour bake):\n\n1. **Base**: fBm of `E.noise2` (5 octaves, lacunarity 2, gain 0.5) with domain warping; amplitude and scale per hunt.\n2. **Features**, in order, from the hunt's data (coordinates in metres in the core):\n   - `ridge { path, height, width }`: a raised Gaussian profile along a Catmull-Rom spline (`E.Path`).\n   - `river { path, width, depth, banks }`: carves a channel with sloped banks; marks water.\n   - `lake { center, radii, depth, level }`: a bowl; marks water at `level`.\n   - `flatten { poly, z: number | 'avg', falloff }`: meadows, fields, the farmyard.\n   - `field { poly, crop: 'corn', rowsDeg }`: ground type and row texture; corn-row props scattered by rule.\n   - `trail { path, width }`: slight flattening, a dirt ground type; the walk-in paths follow trails.\n   - `clearView { vantage, bearings: [a, b], dist: [near, far] }`: lowers any terrain between the vantage's eye and\n     the fan's far edge so nothing unplanned blocks the play area (authored hills in the fan stay, under a margin).\n   - `snow { above, amount }`, `burn { poly }`: ground types.\n3. **Water**: level and mask; a lake or river surface is drawn at its level wherever the ground is below it.\n4. **Ground types** by rules (slope, height, distance to water, masks, noise): grass, dry grass, leaf litter, dirt,\n   rock, talus, mud, reeds, snow, ice, corn, burn. Each has a macro colour and a micro tile.\n5. **Light** (bake): normals from central differences; Lambert with the hunt's sun direction and colours; a cheap\n   ambient occlusion (height minus a blurred height); sun shadows by marching toward the sun in 2 m steps; tree shadows\n   stamped as soft ellipses offset along the sun direction after scatter. Dithered into the colour map.\n6. **Scatter** (`E.gen.scatter` with the `world` seed): forests, groves and single trees, bushes, rocks, logs, stumps,\n   reeds, tall grass, corn rows, snowdrifts, by mask rules per hunt; never inside a site's play fan except authored\n   cover. Each placed prop registers its occluder volumes in a 32 m spatial grid (5.9).\n\nSampling: `height(x, y)` bilinear (core, else far ring); `color(x, y)`; `type(x, y)`; `normal(x, y)`;\n`water(x, y)` gives the surface level or null; `rayHit(origin, dir, maxD)` marches 0.5 m steps (growing with distance)\nthen bisects.\n\n**Tests** (unit/terrain): a hunt's generation hash is stable for its seed; a river's bed is at least its depth below\nthe banks; lake water level and mask agree; `clearView` leaves the fan's sightline clear (rays from the eye to 50\nsample points in the fan reach their target); `rayHit` agrees with brute-force sampling within 0.1 m.\n\n### 5.3 The renderer (`vista.js`: `E.Vista`)\n\n`vista.draw(r, lens, opts)` draws a full frame into the game buffer as one `r.overlay(g => ...)`. `vista.render(g, lens,\nrect)` is the same pass into any rectangle (the scope lens, the Trophy Cam, the title).\n\n1. **Sky and Backdrop.** An `E.Backdrop` per hunt (sky gradient, stars, cloud and fog layers, far mountain layers)\n   drawn above the horizon through a stand-in renderer whose `ix = yaw * f * 10` so it turns with the camera (as\n   `mode7.js drawSky` does). Our own sun or moon disc with a dithered glow is placed by its world bearing and elevation\n   (not the Backdrop's screen-fixed sun). Dusk adds dithered additive god rays.\n2. **Terrain raster** (front to back, one column at a time, into a `Uint32Array` frame with alpha 0 for sky, then\n   `putImageData` into a scratch canvas drawn over the sky):\n   - for column `sx`: ground direction `F + R*((sx - cx)/f)`; march D from 1 m with step `max(0.15 m, D*0.0075)` to\n     the core's edge, then the far ring with coarser steps, to the hunt's far distance (about 3 km), stopping when\n     the column is full;\n   - at each step, sample height `h`; `y = horizonY - (h - C.z)*f/D`; if `y` is above the column's current top (the\n     column fills from the bottom of the screen upward), fill from `y` down to that top with the ground colour, shaded\n     by distance fog (`fogNear..fogFar`) and height fog (valley mist below `mistTop`), and for near spans (D < 25 m)\n     with per-pixel micro-texture (the pixel's ground point found on the plane of that sample) and ordered dither;\n     the column's top becomes `y`;\n   - record an **occlusion breakpoint** `(D, y)` each time the column's top changes (typed arrays, up to 512 per\n     column): `occY(col, D)` is the topmost terrain pixel drawn by samples nearer than D;\n   - water: where the ground is under the water level, draw the surface at the level: the sky colour at the mirrored\n     row, darkened and tinted, with animated ripple bands and sun glints; shoreline foam; ice edges in Muskeg.\n3. **Billboards**, gathered each frame, sorted far to near, each `{ x, y, z, D, k, bounds: [w, h], draw }`:\n   - skip if off screen or past the fog's far edge; compute its screen box from its world bounds;\n   - if `occY(col, D - 0.6 m)` is below the box's bottom for every column, draw straight onto the frame;\n     if it is above the box's top everywhere, skip it; otherwise draw it into a screen-sized scratch canvas, clear\n     each column below `occY` (`clearRect(x, occY, 1, ...)`), and draw the box onto the frame. The 0.6 m bias keeps\n     hooves from being clipped by the ground they stand on;\n   - fog: billboards past `fogNear` are drawn through a fog tint (pre-tinted impostor levels, or `px.blend` with the\n     fog colour at stepped alpha).\n4. **Impostors** (`E.Impostors`): props are drawn from sprites pre-rendered at 24 scale levels (k from 0.01 to 3\n   pixels per unit, geometric) and up to 8 facings, picked at the nearest level at or below the needed k and drawn\n   with nearest-neighbour `drawImage` scaling of at most 1.12x (`E.ctx2d` contexts: smoothing off). Library shapes\n   come from `E.shapeSprite(id, { view, facing, size })` with a Vista view at that scale; custom props from our\n   painters `paint(g, k, variant)`. Levels are built lazily and pre-warmed in boot steps for the current hunt. Near\n   trees (D < 40 m) sway: their top rows are drawn shifted by a sine of time (wind).\n5. **Animals and critters** are drawn live each frame: `rig.draw(g, sx, sy, view)` with the cached view for their k\n   and pitch, plus a 1-pixel outline (draw the rig's silhouette offset 4 ways in the outline colour into the scratch\n   canvas, then the rig). The outline colour is per hunt (warm orange at dusk reads as rim light). LOD: below 1.2\n   pixels per unit skip ear, tail and eye details; below 6 pixels tall draw a 3-tone silhouette (but always draw\n   antlers, 5.4).\n6. **World particles** (`12-fx.js`): dust, dirt, bark chips, leaves, water droplets, snow puffs, glass and clay shards,\n   feathers, breath vapour; projected each frame, drawn as `px.rect`/`px.disc` sized by k (at least 1 pixel). Cap 1500.\n7. **Foreground dressing** per vantage, in screen space with slight parallax: tree-stand rail, blind window edges,\n   canoe bow, grass tufts and leaves at the bottom corners. Plus near grass-tuft billboards 3-8 m out that hide\n   hooves.\n8. **Weather and grade**: screen-space snow, leaves or drizzle in three parallax layers (cosmetic RNG); the hunt's\n   colour grade; a light vignette.\n9. **Scope lenses**: for each scoped player, render the world again into a `2R x 2R` scratch canvas with the\n   magnified lens (5.1) about that player's swayed aim point, mask it to a circle (a `px.disc` mask with\n   `destination-in`), draw it at the reticle, then the scope ring, crosshair and breath arc. R = 54 px at 480 x 270.\n\nThe renderer must also be callable with a flat terrain (all heights 0, `opts.flat`) for milestone M1 and as the\nfallback in section 8.\n\n**Tests**: unit/vista (headless): occlusion breakpoints are monotonic per column; `occY` matches a brute-force\nreference for random columns; billboard sort is far to near. browser/vista: no errors; screenshots of each hunt's\nthree vantages at fixed seeds go in `tests/out/` for review; a pick-agreement check (5.9).\n\n### 5.4 Four-legged rigs (`quadruped.js`: `E.Quadruped`)\n\nBuilt like the engine's Humanoid: a 3D skeleton posed by math every step from continuous inputs, drawn as shaded pixel\nvolumes through any `E.View`. API:\n\n```js\nconst rig = new E.Quadruped({ species: 'whitetail', sex: 'buck', size: 1, colors: {...}, antlers: { points: 8, seed: 3 } });\nrig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, ground: (x, y) => h, gait: 'auto', alert: 0..1, graze: 0..1, look: { x, y, z },\n                 hurt: 0..1, limp: legIndex | -1, jump: 0..1, dead: 0..1, call: 0..1 /* bugle or grunt pose */, splash });\nrig.draw(g, ox, oy, view);            // inside a billboard\nrig.J                                 // joints in world space after update (headless too)\nrig.zones()                           // hit volumes in world space (5.9)\nrig.drawXray(g, x, y, scale, hits)    // side-on x-ray for the Shot Chart\nrig.drawMount(g, x, y, size)          // head-and-antlers plaque for the Lodge and HUD icons\nrig.debug(g, view)                    // skeleton overlay for dev mode\n```\n\n**Skeleton** (local frame: x forward, y right, z up, units = world units at `size: 1` for a whitetail; other species\nscale it): pelvis, mid-spine, withers, neck base, neck mid, head, muzzle, ears L/R, tail base and tip; four legs, each\ntop joint (shoulder or hip), elbow or stifle, knee or hock, fetlock, hoof.\n\n| Whitetail rest pose (units; 16 = 1 m) | |\n|---|---|\n| Spine: pelvis -> withers | (-10, 0, 14.5) -> (8, 0, 15.8) (shoulder height 1.0 m) |\n| Torso cross-sections (at x: top z, bottom z, half width) | chest x 5: 15.8, 8.8, 3.2; belly x -2: 14.8, 9.6, 3.4; rump x -9: 14.6, 10.4, 3.0 |\n| Fore leg top (shoulder joint) / hind leg top (hip joint) | (8, +-2.4, 10.8) / (-10, +-2.6, 12) |\n| Fore bones: humerus, forearm, cannon, pastern+hoof | 3.8, 4.6, 3.8, 1.4 (a gentle zigzag at rest) |\n| Hind bones: femur, tibia, cannon, pastern+hoof | 4.6, 5.2, 4.4, 1.4 (stifle forward, hock back) |\n| Neck base -> head | (9, 0, 15.5) -> (14.5, 0, 22.5), about 50 degrees up; head 5.5 long, muzzle tipped 25 degrees down |\n| Ears, tail | ears 2.8 long at the back of the skull; tail base (-11.5, 0, 14.6), 3.5 long |\n\nElk: x1.5 with a longer neck, dark neck mane, pale rump patch. Moose: x1.9, long legs (legs x1.15 relative), humped\nwithers, long heavy head with a dewlap (\"bell\"), short tail. Fawn and calf: x0.6 with big ears, spots on fawns.\nCanid preset (fox, coyote, wolf): digitigrade legs, bushy tail, pointed ears. Cow preset (Bessie): bulky, black and\nwhite patches, a bell.\n\n**Gaits** (per-leg phase offsets [LF, RF, LH, RH] and stance share of the cycle):\n\n| Gait | Offsets | Stance | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| walk | 0.25, 0.75, 0, 0.5 | 0.65 | 4-beat; head nods with each fore step |\n| trot | 0, 0.5, 0.5, 0 | 0.45 | diagonal pairs; small body bob |\n| gallop | 0.5, 0.6, 0, 0.1 | 0.30 | spine flexes +-5%, body pitches, a flight phase |\n| bound | 0.45, 0.5, 0, 0.05 | 0.25 | the whitetail escape: big arcs, tail flagged up showing white |\n\nStride length grows with speed; the cycle frequency is speed / stride. `gait: 'auto'` picks by speed with blending.\nHooves in stance are locked to the ground point where they touched down (no sliding); in swing they arc from lift-off\nto the predicted next touchdown (body position + velocity x the remaining swing and half a stance). Leg solve: the\nfetlock target sits above the hoof; the cannon bone's angle comes from the phase (upright in stance, folded in\nswing: fore cannons fold back, hind cannons angle forward); then `E.ik3` from the top joint to the knee or hock with\nthe bend hint (fore elbows bend back, hind stifles bend forward). Body pitch and roll follow the ground under the four\nhooves (`ground(x, y)`); the heading turns toward the velocity with a limit, leaning into turns.\n\n**Poses** (continuous weights, blended): idle (breathing, weight shifts, ear flicks, tail swish), graze (neck to the\nground, jaw chewing), alert (head high, ears forward toward `look`, a foreleg stomp now and then), look-at (head and\nneck turn within limits), startle (crouch, then spring), jump (legs tucked over a log, fence or creek), call (elk:\nneck stretched and mouth open while the bugle sounds; moose: head low, grunting), hurt (a flinch: body roll, head\ntoss, 0.25 s), limp (one leg's stride cut to 40%), death (1.0 s: front knees buckle, chest drops, hind legs give, the\nbody rolls about 70 degrees onto its side away from the shot, the head settles last), dead (lying, still).\n\n**Antlers** (`E.Antlers`): generated 3D polylines from a seed, drawn after the head with 1-3 pixel thickness by k,\nbone-coloured with darker bases and a dark outline:\n- whitetail: main beams sweep back, out and forward; tines rise from the beam; brow tines; `points` 4-12 (typical);\n  the trophy has 14 with kickers and a drop tine;\n- elk: long beams sweeping back, 5-7 tines per side, a long dagger fourth tine (the trophy is 7 x 7);\n- moose: palmate paddles with points along the rim, spread 40-55 inches (the trophy 72 inches).\nReadability: every animal is drawn (and zoned) at `animalScale` 1.1, an arcade size boost (dev slider); antlers are\ndrawn at x1.15 more (dev slider) and grow with distance, x(1 + max(0, (D - 80 m) / 150 m)) capped at 1.6, so bucks\nread at range; an optional assist setting adds a one-pixel glint on antler tips.\n\n**Tests** (unit/quadruped): every joint is finite through each gait at several speeds; planted hooves move less than\n0.5 units during stance; the death pose ends with the pelvis below 40% of standing height; zones follow joints\n(the heart sphere stays inside the chest); antler generation is deterministic per seed and has the requested points.\nVisual: `tools/filmstrip.mjs` strips of walk, trot, gallop, bound, graze-to-alert, death for each species, reviewed by\neye (the one thing a script can't judge), from the Animal Lab deep link.\n\n### 5.5 Critters, people and other rigs\n- **Small critters** use the engine's `E.Blob` (feet, ears, tail, wings, mouth options) plus small extra drawing (a\n  raccoon's mask and ringed tail, a skunk's stripe, a beaver's flat tail, a turkey's fan and wattle).\n- **Birds** (crows, grouse, ducks, ravens, eagle, loons): Blob with wings and `flap`; flight on arcs and `E.Path`s.\n- **Canids and the cow** use `E.Quadruped` presets.\n- **People** are `E.Humanoid`s (size 1.3): Uncle Dub (bulky, blaze-orange cap, canvas coat, grey beard), Granny Mae\n  (chibi, grey bun, shawl, rolling pin), Cousin Dwayne (heroic, mirrored shades, camo), Loretta Pruitt (heroic,\n  ponytail, red flannel), Big Earl (bulky, overalls, chili pot), the Emcee (cap, bow tie, microphone). They appear as\n  dialog portraits (`talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`), in the Lodge, and in-world in bonus games (Granny on\n  her porch; Loretta at the trap house; Earl by the lake fire). Use their engine poses: `wave`, `cheer`, `hips`\n  (Dwayne's taunt), `throw` (Granny's crow-scaring rolling pin wave), `kneel`.\n- **Snowmen** (bonus): stacked shaded discs with stick arms and hats, a throw animation, a topple.\n\n### 5.6 Animal AI (`30-actors.js`, `31-patterns.js`)\n\nEach animal is an actor `{ id, species, sex, rig, x, y, z, vx, vy, heading, state, orders[], hp, fear, wounded,\nbagger, ... }`. States: `hidden` (behind cover, not yet out), `enter`, `walk`, `trot`, `run`, `graze`, `look`, `stop`,\n`jump`, `wade`, `wounded`, `dying`, `dead`, `exit`, `gone`.\n\n- **Orders** come from spawn patterns, as a queue: `['walk', { to: 0.4 }]`, `['graze', 3]`, `['look', 1.5]`,\n  `['trot', { to: 0.8 }]`, `['run', { to: 1 }]`, `['exit']`. Movement follows the actor's path (an `E.Path` of\n  terrain points; z from the heightfield); path points can carry `jump` marks (logs, fences, the creek) where the rig\n  leaps.\n- **Herds**: followers trail their leader's path at `[back, side]` offsets with 0.1-0.4 s reaction delays (from the\n  `ai` stream) and keep 1.5 m apart.\n- **Senses and spooking**: a gunshot emits `noise` at the shooter (loudness 1, heard to 400 m) and at the impact\n  (0.6 within 40 m). Each animal adds fear by loudness and distance; past its threshold it startles (alert 0.15-0.45 s),\n  then flees along its path (or the pattern's escape route) at run speed. A near miss (impact within 4 m) bolts it at\n  once with a jump. Fleeing animals that hear more shots speed up 10%. Species temper: whitetail skittish (threshold\n  0.35), elk herd-minded (the herd flees together), moose stubborn (threshold 0.7; may stop and stare instead).\n- **Calls** (5.8): within the call's radius, animals that are not panicking stop and look toward the caller for\n  `callFreeze` seconds (1.4 +- 0.2), then go on (moose may take a few steps toward you). Each animal answers a call at\n  most twice per site. Bull elk may bugle back. Trophies have their own rules (6.3).\n- **Wounds and death**: zone damage (5.9) reduces hp; at 0 it dies. A wounded animal limps (speed x0.6, the hit\n  leg's stride cut) and heads for the nearest exit; further hits take more hp; if it leaves the frame it is **LOST**\n  (a penalty). A killed\n  animal plays `dying` then lies `dead` with a tag over it (the bagger's colour, its points) until the site ends.\n- **Exits**: an animal past its path's end or outside the camera's bearing range plus 10 degrees is `gone`; a buck\n  that goes is **ESCAPED** (its HUD icon is crossed out).\n- **Visibility** (for quick-shot bonuses and the bot): on screen, and an analytic ray from the camera to its chest is\n  clear of terrain and occluders.\n\n**Spawn patterns** (data; each places one or more actors with paths in site-relative `[bearing, dist]` points and\norder queues; variations from the `spawn` stream):\n\n| Pattern | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `cross(path, gait)` | enters at the path's start, crosses, exits |\n| `emerge(cover, path)` | waits hidden behind a cover prop, steps out alert, then goes on |\n| `graze(point, secs)` | walks in, grazes with glances up, wanders off |\n| `herdPass(path, n, spacing)` | a leader and followers on one path |\n| `ridgeRun(path)` | a fast run along a crest, silhouetted against the sky |\n| `wade(path)` | moves through water: slow, splashing, legs hidden below the surface |\n| `bolt(bed, dir)` | bedded down unseen; jumps up and runs at the first shot or after a time |\n| `decoyDoe(buck)` | a doe timed to cross in front of a buck (patience test) |\n| `stand(point, secs)` | stops broadside in the open (an easy shot, used sparingly) |\n| `charge(target)` | runs straight at the vantage (Big Mo, the timberline bull) |\n| `fight(a, b, secs)` | two bulls lock antlers, then split |\n\n**Tests** (unit/ai): a shot within hearing makes calm animals flee within 0.5 s; a near miss bolts at once; calls\nfreeze non-panicking animals for 1.2-1.6 s and never more than twice each; followers keep spacing; a wounded buck that\nexits counts as lost; a killed buck stays dead and tagged; every pattern runs to `gone` or `dead` without errors.\n\n### 5.7 Sites (`32-site.js`)\n\nA site is data plus a script:\n\n```js\ndef('site', 'hollow-1', {\n  hunt: 'hollow', name: 'Creek Crossing', vantage: 'creekStand',       // the hunt's vantage: x, y (m), eye height, base yaw\n  frame: { bearings: [-38, 38], fov: 34, pitch: 3 },                  // the play fan; camera limits\n  director: [[0, { bearing: -12 }], [6, { bearing: 4, dur: 8, ease: 'inOutSine' }], [30, { mode: 'follow' }]],\n  time: 60, bucks: 3,                                                 // bucks scheduled (the HUD shows them)\n  props: [['log', [-20, 32]], ['bush', [14, 28]]],                     // authored cover, [bearing, dist]\n  script: function* (S) {                                             // a game.script coroutine; S = the site API\n    yield 1.2; S.bark('start');\n    S.pattern('cross', { kind: 'doe', path: [[-35, 44], [-5, 36], [30, 46]], gait: 'walk' });\n    S.pattern('cross', { kind: 'fawn', follow: S.last, back: 2.5 });\n    yield 5;\n    S.pattern('graze', { kind: 'buck', points: S.rng.int(5, 8), at: [10, 34], secs: 4 });\n    yield E.any([E.until(() => S.bucksLeft <= 2), 14]);\n    S.pattern('herdPass', { kinds: ['buck', 'doe', 'buck'], path: [[38, 28], [0, 24], [-40, 40]], gait: 'bound', splash: true });\n    yield E.until(() => S.allResolved);                               // every buck spawned so far bagged, escaped or lost\n  },\n  critters: [['squirrel', { tree: [-30, 22], at: 8 }], ['rabbit', { path: [[20, 15], [5, 12]], at: 20 }]]\n});\n```\n\n- `S` gives: `pattern`, `spawn`, `bark`, `say` (captions), `rng` (with `int`, `pick`, `chance`), `time`, `bucksLeft`,\n  `actors`, `players`, `on(event, fn)` (bus events such as calls and shots, cleared when the site ends),\n  `allResolved`, `last` (the last actor spawned).\n- **End rules**: the site ends when every scheduled buck is bagged, escaped or lost and the script has finished, or\n  when time runs out (TIME!: the rest flee), or when every player is benched. Then `siteEnd` (1.5 s of the last\n  animals running off), then the tally.\n- In AI Hub rounds and Party Quick Draw, sites are **time-boxed**: they run their full time and their scripts loop\n  their waves; bucks keep coming.\n- Results per player: shots, hits, kills by zone, distances, bonuses, penalties, stars, the Shot Chart records.\n\n**Tests** (flow/sites): for each site at seeds 1-3: the bot (cheat aim) bags every buck and the site ends early with a\nclean sweep; with no shots the site ends on time with every buck escaped; shooting the first doe benches the player\nand (single player) ends the site; the bucks scheduled equal the bucks spawned; no errors.\n\n### 5.8 Rifle, scope and call (`40-weapon.js`) and aiming (`41-aim.js`)\n\n**Rifle** (each player): `rounds` left (5 when full; dev slider 1-10) and a `chambered` flag. A site starts with 5\nrounds and one chambered. The HUD shows 5 shell icons (rounds) and a chamber light.\n- Fire: needs a chambered round and 0.1 s since the last shot. It spends a round and empties the chamber.\n  Fire with an empty chamber: a dry click, the reticle shakes, and the PUMP prompt flashes (a bark the first two times\n  per hunt).\n- Pump: with rounds left, racks in 0.22 s (two clicks) and chambers the next one. With none left, the pump reloads:\n  1.25 s, five shell clicks, 5 rounds and one chambered. So a tube is 5 shots. Pumping while racking or reloading does\n  nothing.\n- **Auto-pump** setting (off by default; Easy difficulty and the attract bot turn it on) racks 0.12 s after each shot.\n- Bonus games use their own tube (10-12) and a faster rack (0.15 s).\n\n**Scope** (hold, or toggle by setting): raises in 0.15 s; draws the lens (5.3 step 9) at x3.5 magnification (dev\nslider 1.5-6); pad reticle speed x0.45 while scoped. **Sway**: the aim point drifts by\n`A * (0.6 sin 1.3t + 0.4 sin(2.9t+1), 0.5 sin(1.7t+2) + 0.3 sin 3.3t)` screen pixels, `A = 2.6` (slider). **Hold\nbreath** (held): sway x0.15 for up to 3.0 s (slider), the meter refills at 1.2 per second after letting go; emptying it\nforces an exhale and doubles sway for 1.5 s. The lens view drifts with the sway; its crosshair stays centred; the shot\ngoes through the swayed point.\n\n**Call** (species-specific sound: whitetail grunt, elk bugle, moose grunt): 2 charges per player per site (slider\n0-5), 3 s cooldown, radius 200 m, effect in 5.6. A call is a shared event: in multiplayer, everyone's animals stop.\n\n**Reticle** (each player, in screen pixels of the 480 x 270 buffer):\n- Mouse: the reticle is the pointer (`input.pointer()`), absolute; keeps its place when the pointer leaves.\n- Stick: `input.move()` (left stick, d-pad, and for keyboard-only players WASD and arrows) gives a velocity: max 400\n  px/s (slider), response curve `|v|^1.7`, 0.15 s ramp to full speed; the right stick (`input.padAim`) adds fine aim at\n  x0.3; scoped x0.45.\n- **Aim assist** (pads and keyboard only, never the mouse; setting Off / Light / Strong; default Light): friction x0.65\n  (Light) or x0.45 (Strong) when the reticle is within 8 px of a live target's projected hit volume; Strong also pulls\n  25% of the way toward the vital zone per second while the stick moves toward it. Never toward does or protected\n  critters.\n- Each player's reticle has its own shape as well as colour: P1 circle-cross, P2 diamond, P3 square brackets, P4\n  triangle. Benched players' reticles turn grey with a BENCHED tag.\n\n**Tests** (unit/weapon): fire, pump and reload timings at 1/120 s steps; dry fire with an empty chamber; auto-pump;\nreload refills the tube; sway is deterministic for a time and shrinks while holding breath; breath empties after 3.0\ns and forces the shaky phase; calls spend charges and respect the cooldown. (unit/aim): stick curve and ramp; mouse\nabsolute; assist friction applies to bucks and not to does; scoped speed scale.\n\n### 5.9 Shot resolution and hit zones (`42-shot.js`)\n\nA shot is analytic (it works headless and matches what is drawn): the player's ray (5.1) at the swayed aim point.\nCandidates, nearest forward distance wins:\n1. **Animals**: bounding sphere first; then every zone the ray crosses on that animal (spheres and capsules from\n   `rig.zones()`); the scoring zone is the highest-priority zone crossed on the first animal hit: heart > head >\n   lung > neck > shoulder > gut > ham > leg > antler. A doe or fawn in front of a buck takes the shot.\n2. **Critters**: spheres (and a capsule for long ones).\n3. **Occluders**: tree trunks (vertical cylinders), rocks (spheres), logs (capsules), fence posts, hay bales, the barn,\n   stand and blind frames (boxes); looked up along the ray's ground track in the 32 m grid. Foliage, bushes, reeds,\n   grass and corn do not block shots (they only hide).\n4. **Terrain**: `rayHit` up to the nearest candidate's distance.\n5. **Water**: the surface plane where the mask says water.\n\n| Zone (whitetail at rest, local units) | Volume | Damage | Lethal | Score x | Callout |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| heart | sphere r 1.5 at (6, 0, 10.6): low in the chest, just behind the fore leg | 9 | yes | 2.0 | HEART SHOT! |\n| head | sphere r 2.0 at the skull | 9 | yes | 1.8 | HEADSHOT! |\n| lung | ellipsoid radii (3.6, 2.7, 2.6) at (4.5, 0, 12.2) | 9 | yes | 1.6 | LUNG SHOT! |\n| neck | capsule neck base to head base, r 1.8 | 9 | yes | 1.4 | NECK SHOT! |\n| shoulder | sphere r 2.2 at each fore leg top | 9 | yes | 1.2 | SHOULDER! |\n| gut | capsule (3, 0, 12.2) to (-8, 0, 12.4), r 3.1 | 0.6 | no | 0.7 | GUT SHOT |\n| ham | sphere r 3.0 at (-9, 0, 12.4) | 0.5 | no | 0.6 | HAM HIT |\n| leg | a capsule per bone, r 1.1 (upper bones) and 0.8 (lower) | 0.4 | no | 0.5 | LEG HIT |\n| antler | capsules along beams and tines, r 0.35 | 0 | no | 0 | ANTLER PING! (no damage; spooks) |\n\nZones are rebuilt from the joints every step (they bend, run and fall with the rig) and every zone stays inside the\ndrawn body, so a shot just off the silhouette misses.\n\nHit points: whitetail 1, elk 1.5, moose 2 (a lethal zone kills any of them; a deer drops to two gut shots, a moose to\nfour). Elk and moose zones scale with the rig.\nEffects by what was hit: fur puff and thump (animal), feathers (bird), bark chips and a wood knock (trunk), sparks and a\nricochet whine (rock), dirt clods and a thud (terrain), a splash column (water), nothing but the echo (sky). Every shot\nemits spook noise (5.6). Same-step hits on one animal by two players: the better zone gets it; equal zones share the\nkill (SHARED! half points each).\n\nHits are stored in the animal's local frame (position, heading, body pitch and roll at the moment) for the Shot Chart.\n\n**Tests** (unit/shot): rays through each zone's centre on a posed whitetail, elk and moose return that zone; a ray\nthrough the heart from the side passes the lung first and still scores heart; a trunk in front blocks; a crest in\nfront blocks; a doe in front of a buck takes the shot; antler ping does no damage; water plane splash; equal-zone\nsame-step hits share. **browser/pick** (the WYSIWYG check): render each live animal of a busy scene as a flat ID\ncolour into an offscreen canvas, sample 300 pixels inside the silhouettes (not antlers) and cast analytic rays: at\nleast 95% must hit the animal drawn there; sample 300 pixels 3 px outside the silhouettes: at most 5% may hit it.\n\n### 5.10 Scoring (`43-score.js`)\n\n```\nkill points = round( base(species, antlers) x distanceMult x zoneMult x (running ? 1.25 : 1) x (trophy ? 3 : 1) ) + quick + multi\nrunning = the animal was moving faster than its species' trot speed when hit; zoneMult = the killing shot's zone\nbase: whitetail 100 + 15 per point; elk 150 + 20 per point; moose 200 + 6 per inch of spread\ndistanceMult = clamp(1 + (metres - 25) / 75, 1, 3.5)          (100 m = 2.0; 175 m = 3.0)\nquick = +50 if the kill comes within 1.0 s of the buck first being visible\nmulti = DOUBLE! +250 for a second kill within 1.5 s, TRIPLE! +750 for a third\n```\n\nSite bonuses: **Accuracy** `round(hits / shots x 1000)` when at least 50% (hits on any target count; antler pings\ndon't); **Clean sweep** +1000 (every buck bagged, every kill one shot in a lethal zone); **No penalties** +200.\nCritters: fixed points (6.7). Penalties: doe or fawn: benched for the site (Classic) or -300 (Casual); skunk -100 and a\ngreen stink cloud over the screen for 3 s; Bessie -500; eagle or loon -400; a lost wounded animal -100. Scores never go\nbelow 0 per site.\n\n**Stars** per site (antlers, 1-3): 1 for bagging a buck, 2 for bagging every buck, 3 for a clean sweep with no\npenalties. **Rank** after a hunt, against its par (tuned to the bot's average): Greenhorn < 40%, Tenderfoot < 60%,\nWoodsman < 80%, Sharpshooter < 100%, Big Game Pro < 125%, Legend of the Lodge from 125%.\n\n**Rivals** (Hunt-Off campaign): Big Earl, Loretta Pruitt and Cousin Dwayne post preset scores per hunt (start at 55%,\n85% and 100% of par; tune). The HUD shows Dwayne's score for the hunt as the one to beat; beating him gives +1000\nBRAGGING RIGHTS and a grumble.\n\n**Tests** (unit/score): the formula for given inputs (table-driven); multi-kill windows; accuracy threshold; clean\nsweep only with one-shot lethal kills; penalties per setting; stars; ranks at the boundaries; scores never negative.\n\n### 5.11 Players and local multiplayer (`50-players.js`)\n- `game.players` (engine): P1 is `game.input` (mouse and keyboard, or a pad); players 2-4 join with Options/Start on a\n  free pad (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`) on the Party Hunt join page; a second keyboard half\n  (`keys: 'right'`, arrows to aim) is allowed but not advertised.\n- Colours are the AI Hub seat colours: P1 `#e8443a` red, P2 `#3b82f6` blue, P3 `#22c55e` green, P4 `#f5b700` gold,\n  plus the reticle shapes (5.8) so colour is never the only cue.","numLines":420,"startLine":380,"totalLines":1563}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01X6MbmA8BHAcxhDsf8roob8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
