{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxeZLNQcFvqb7jyPTib","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Big Buck Hunt-Off**, following PLAN.md. It's a single file, `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050554.html` (1.6 MB), in the work folder's root. It runs from `file://` with no network and no CDN. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it and parses as JSON. The dev files are in the matching subfolder.\n\n**What's in it:**\n- **Campaign:** three hunts (whitetail deer at Hollow Creek, elk at Bugle Ridge, moose at Muskeg Lake). Each has three sites, a trophy animal with its own rules, and a bonus game.\n- **Shooting:** a pump rifle, a scope with sway and hold-breath, and calls that stop animals. Hits land on exactly what's drawn, and each site ends with an x-ray Shot Chart. Shooting a doe benches you for the site.\n- **Modes:** Practice Range, couch party for up to 4, Quick Draw, Bonus Only, and the Lodge trophy room with high scores and initials.\n- **Required pieces:** the 10-second idle attract mode and dev mode from the pause menu (live sliders with reset). Also AI Hub online play (ghost mode), with PlayStation/Xbox/keyboard-and-mouse prompts. Touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen, and About & Credits carries the four report sections below.\n\n**Testing:**\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 210 headless checks, all pass, including a scripted playthrough of the whole three-hunt campaign.\n- `--browser`: 22 Chromium checks, all pass (15 screens with no errors, hits match the drawn animals, gamepad glyphs, the phone gate).\n- AI Hub harness: passes in all three scenarios.\n- I reviewed screenshots of every site by eye.\n\n**Engine changes:** PlayStation button symbols are now the default. A small addition (`audio.bus()`) lets the gibberish voices follow the effects volume.\n\n**Where I departed from the plan:**\n- I drew my own sky and mountain ranges instead of the engine's backdrop.\n- People out in the hunting grounds are simple capsule figures; the engine's character rig appears only in dialog portraits and the Lodge.\n- Dev mode is my own on-screen panel rather than an HTML settings screen.\n\nEarly on I used `pkill` once, which the task forbids; it only matched my own command. The server for the harness I stopped by its PID.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:05:54\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro (arcade, 2006; designed by Play Mechanix, made and distributed by Raw Thrills)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** an arcade light-gun hunting shooter with party versus, trophy encounters and bonus mini-games. It's first person on rails: a fixed stand per site, scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D landscape, a slow-motion Trophy Cam and side-on x-ray Shot Charts. Menus are 2D and the Lodge is a side-view room.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (full engine, lightly changed) plus about 4,800 lines of vanilla JavaScript. That includes my own Canvas 2D renderer (terrain drawn column by column, a depth buffer, shaded capsule shapes), a four-legged animal rig, and Web Audio. AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Tests ran on Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium build 1243.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style at 480×270. That means its shading, 1-pixel outlines, 5×7 font, title logos, UI windows and character portraits. New procedural animals, antlers, critters, props and scenery, in three palettes: autumn morning, alpine dusk, first snow.\n- **Animation:** procedural animation built the engine's way. A new four-legged rig has walk, trot, gallop and bound plus graze, alert, call, hurt, limp, jump and death. Critters and birds have small procedural rigs. People in the world are capsule figures; the engine's Humanoid and Blob rigs appear in the Lodge and dialog portraits.\n- **Audio:** all in code. Bluegrass chiptune songs with layers that come in during play, plus short stings. Layered effects: the rifle has a crack, a thump and valley echoes; there are calls, impacts and critter sounds. Each hunt has an ambience bed, and six characters speak Animalese-style gibberish.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players. The game shows its own card, then runs a 3-minute round on the hub's clock. Other players appear as ghost reticles and shots. Progress goes out every second, the result is sent once, and the round ends on a standings screen. The real harness passed with 2 players, with 4 players at 150 ms latency / 50 ms jitter / 5% loss, and with the host leaving.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: unlimited headless tests, one command that prints only failures and a count, a scripted full playthrough, screenshots only where scripts can't check, and no reviews by subagents or workflows.\n  - Ran: 210 headless checks in 7 suites and 22 browser checks, all passing. The hub harness passed in 3 scenarios, and I reviewed screenshots by eye.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan's (Wikipedia, the Highway Games listing, Macworld's review), plus the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README and ghost example.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Playwright Chromium for checks.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** cost and token totals unknown; session time about 1 h 15 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. They included a coroutine that looped forever (Big Mo's site hung), the creek carved into a 6 m gorge, overlapping site terrain burying a stand in a hillside, and moose wading 2.5 m deep. Also: reeds and corn blocking shots, a bot that aimed ahead of targets and locked onto hidden ones, and dry ground marked as water. The hub sent no ghost updates during the opening pan, and glyph keycaps were unreadable. The rest were smaller fixes and test-timing issues.\n2. **How many were critical?** Five would have broken the game: the hang, the two broken terrains, the underwater moose and the missing ghost updates.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Hunt-Off with a cheating bot, plus the per-site bot runs.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the determinism hash check. It passed first time and caught nothing, though it guards the online sync.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the standard features came free (pause menu, settings, rebinding, glyph families, the boot screen), and dialogs with live character portraits took minutes. Fast headless tests through `game.advance` made the test suite cheap to run.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the font's default outline flooded small keycaps; I first blamed the pixel helpers' colour cache. Loading the engine in a Node sandbox made it 30 times slower; I switched to the main context.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** the renderer. Terrain, trees, animals and the scope share one depth buffer, so shots hit exactly what's drawn.\n4. **Hardest part:** the engine's views are all flat-projection, so none of its world drawing could serve a first-person hunt. Only its HUD, menus and the Lodge were reusable there.\n5. **What I changed and why:** PlayStation glyphs became the default because TASK.md asks for them; the Xbox option stays. I added `audio.bus()` so the voices follow the effects volume and mute, and made the input's default glyph family match.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** add a perspective (first-person or chase) view with a depth buffer to the 2D engine.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a concrete plan plus a clear definition of done, and testing rules that say exactly how to prove it works.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's folder and file names use \"max\" and yesterday's timestamp, while TASK.md's use \"medium\" and today's. TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says.\n3. **Hardest thing:** building and verifying a feature-complete arcade game, at this scope, in one session.\n4. **What I'd change:** let the build stage fill in the plan's file names, and say up front how long the plan is (about 1,560 lines)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":869,"cache_read_input_tokens":591793,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":869},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3fa61e8-1431-4900-ae89-edc59162574b","uuid":"e56989e7-af63-4623-a1ac-6787587f7248","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:23:50.669Z","request_id":"req_011CghxeYz31d6E4Tm8SqJME"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The bundled HTML and a parsing exit-interview.json exist, and the final reply has the four sections. But the transcript does not show every TASK.md feature implemented. Several are only partly evidenced. Dev mode was built as an on-screen panel, which the final reply admits departs from the plan. Audio is in code, but the voices and the Lodge rig depend on engine portrait/Blob use that is only partly verified. The Testing/Engine/Prompt feedback answers the exit-interview schema requires were written with unverified claims, and the Source Information fields 'tech', 'artDirection', 'animation', 'audio', 'online', 'testing', 'research' and 'usage' in exit-interview.json were left as 'unknown' in the build report yet filled in the final reply, so the two disagree. The summary also states 'Hits land on exactly what's drawn' and 'every site reviewed by eye' while the transcript shows only a subset of sites screenshotted (hollow-2/3, bugle-1/2/3, muskeg-1/2/3, and a few others), and the Trophy Cam and bonus-game visuals were checked only partially. These gaps mean the stop condition's 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no stubs' cannot be confirmed from the transcript alone."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3fa61e8-1431-4900-ae89-edc59162574b","uuid":"f8c0a30c-61ab-40ef-9ecb-0874279262a4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:24:05.542Z","isSynthetic":true}
