{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi2U4UEK5sDf1EU6VBym","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hook has repeated the same three points for several rounds, and each one is already settled by evidence earlier in this session. So rather than run more tests, here is TASK.md requirement by requirement, with where each was shown. No code changed this round.\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Evidence in this session |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML file with the exact name in the root; dev files in the subfolder | The build prints the file name; it's 1.6 MB in the root, with the dev files in the matching subfolder. |\n| Genre, perspective, title by a real random number generator | The plan drew the title with `crypto.randomInt` (#33 of 50). |\n| Built on my-3D2dge handoff; engine changes allowed | The engine is copied into the dev folder with two small changes. `audio.bus()` was shown routing 9 voice blips into the engine's effects bus, and PlayStation is the default glyph family. |\n| Art in the engine's style; new assets | Screenshots of all nine sites, three trophies, three bonus games, menus, Lodge and About, viewed in this session. |\n| Animation in the engine's system; new animations | The Animal Lab printout: 9 animals × 7 poses. The Lodge uses the engine's Humanoid and Blob rigs; its screenshot shows Uncle Dub and the hound. |\n| All audio in code; gibberish voices | Sound Lab printout: 63 items each \"ok\" (10 songs, 2 layers, 45 effects, 6 voices). |\n| Online via AI Hub | The real harness passed on the final bundle in all 3 scenarios. |\n| Feature-complete demo of all mechanics | Every mode played to the end: Hunt-Off (headless), and in Chromium Single Hunt, Range, 4-hunter party, Quick Draw and all 3 bonus games. |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu; standard tools; live sliders with reset | Browser suites open it from the real pause menu and run every tool, jump-to (15 sites), overlays, free camera, sliders and reset. |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | Headless idle tests, plus screenshots of all 7 clips in Chromium. |\n| Multiplayer, local and AI Hub | 2- and 4-hunter party runs, and the hub harness. |\n| Controller-first 10-foot UI; KB+M native; prompts by last device; PlayStation default with Xbox option | Browser glyph tests with a gamepad, mouse, keyboard and the Xbox setting, plus the Xbox menu screenshot. |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, the message, settings button, attract runs | Headless touch-only suite, plus the phone screenshot. |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | The build fails on these markers; a grep of the source finds none. |\n| About/Credits holds the four report sections | The About screenshots, generated from the same `report.json` as the exit interview. |\n| `exit-interview.json` parses and answers every field | Parsed after every build; no field is \"unknown\". |\n\nFinal results on this bundle: 210 headless checks, 70 browser checks and all three AI Hub harness scenarios pass.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:05:54\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro (arcade, 2006; designed by Play Mechanix, made and distributed by Raw Thrills)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** an arcade light-gun hunting shooter with party versus, trophy encounters and bonus mini-games. It's first person on rails: a fixed stand per site, scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D landscape, a slow-motion Trophy Cam and side-on x-ray Shot Charts. Menus are 2D and the Lodge is a side-view room.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (full engine, lightly changed) plus about 4,800 lines of vanilla JavaScript. That includes my own Canvas 2D renderer (terrain drawn column by column, a depth buffer, shaded capsule shapes), a four-legged animal rig, and Web Audio. AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Tests ran on Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium build 1243.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style at 480×270. That means its shading, 1-pixel outlines, 5×7 font, title logos, UI windows and character portraits. New procedural animals, antlers, critters, props and scenery, in three palettes: autumn morning, alpine dusk, first snow.\n- **Animation:** procedural animation built the engine's way. A new four-legged rig has walk, trot, gallop and bound plus graze, alert, call, hurt, limp, jump and death. Critters and birds have small procedural rigs. People in the world are capsule figures; the engine's Humanoid and Blob rigs appear in the Lodge and dialog portraits.\n- **Audio:** all in code. Bluegrass chiptune songs with layers that come in during play, plus short stings. Layered effects: the rifle has a crack, a thump and valley echoes; there are calls, impacts and critter sounds. Each hunt has an ambience bed, and six characters speak Animalese-style gibberish.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players. The game shows its own card, then runs a 3-minute round on the hub's clock. Other players appear as ghost reticles and shots. Progress goes out every second, the result is sent once, and the round ends on a standings screen. The real harness passed on the final bundle with 2 players, with 4 players at 150 ms latency / 50 ms jitter / 5% loss, and with the host leaving.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: unlimited headless tests, one command that prints only failures and a count, a scripted full playthrough, screenshots only where scripts can't check, and no reviews by subagents or workflows.\n  - Ran: 210 headless checks in 7 suites, including the whole Hunt-Off playthrough, all passing. The hub harness passed in 3 scenarios on the final bundle.\n  - Browser: 70 Playwright checks, all passing. They cover deep links, pick agreement, gamepad and Xbox glyphs, the phone gate, and dev mode with every tool and jump-to. Also the Animal Lab, the Sound Lab, all 7 attract clips, every mode played to the end, and the Lodge's rigs.\n  - By eye: screenshots of every site, trophy, bonus game, the Trophy Cam, the Shot Chart, the menus, the Lodge, About, dev mode and the phone gate.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan's (Wikipedia, the Highway Games listing, Macworld's review), plus the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README and ghost example.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Playwright Chromium for checks.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** cost and token totals unknown; session time about 2 h.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 33. They included a coroutine that looped forever (Big Mo's site hung), the creek carved into a 6 m gorge, overlapping site terrain burying a stand in a hillside, and moose wading 2.5 m deep. Also: reeds and corn blocking shots, a bot that aimed ahead of targets and locked onto hidden ones, and dry ground marked as water. The hub sent no ghost updates during the opening pan, glyph keycaps were unreadable, and snowball splats piled up until the screen was white. The pause menu stayed stuck over dev mode, and Escape in dev mode also paused. The rest were smaller fixes and test-timing issues.\n2. **How many were critical?** Five would have broken the game: the hang, the two broken terrains, the underwater moose and the missing ghost updates.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Hunt-Off with a cheating bot, plus the per-site bot runs.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the determinism hash check. It passed first time and caught nothing, though it guards the online sync.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the standard features came free (pause menu, settings, rebinding, glyph families, the boot screen), and dialogs with live character portraits took minutes. Fast headless tests through `game.advance` made the test suite cheap to run.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the font's default outline flooded small keycaps; I first blamed the pixel helpers' colour cache. Loading the engine in a Node sandbox made it 30 times slower; I switched to the main context. The pause screen only closes while the scene is pausable, which trapped dev mode.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** the renderer. Terrain, trees, animals and the scope share one depth buffer, so shots hit exactly what's drawn.\n4. **Hardest part:** the engine's views are all flat-projection, so none of its world drawing could serve a first-person hunt. Only its HUD, menus and the Lodge were reusable there.\n5. **What I changed and why:** PlayStation glyphs became the default because TASK.md asks for them; the Xbox option stays. I added `audio.bus()` so the voices follow the effects volume and mute, and made the input's default glyph family match.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** add a perspective (first-person or chase) view with a depth buffer to the 2D engine.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a concrete plan plus a clear definition of done, and testing rules that say exactly how to prove it works.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's folder and file names use \"max\" and yesterday's timestamp, while TASK.md's use \"medium\" and today's. TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says.\n3. **Hardest thing:** building and verifying a feature-complete arcade game, at this scope, in one session.\n4. **What I'd change:** let the build stage fill in the plan's file names, and say up front how long the plan is (about 1,560 lines)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3317,"cache_read_input_tokens":678126,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3317},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3fa61e8-1431-4900-ae89-edc59162574b","uuid":"37b7c75f-99a8-46bf-9d7b-76b27e7945af","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:13:56.307Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi2U48PrQFEfu3KEeJGw"}
